    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a schema for a retail app. we're currently in 2nf but i'm starting to see some issues with redundancy and update anomalies. can you walk me through the steps to get this into 3nf? specifically how to identify transitive dependencies that need to be moved out.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! The first step to normalize your schema to Third Normal Form (3NF) is to identify any transitive dependencies. A transitive dependency occurs when non-prime attributes (those not part of a candidate key) depend on other non-prime attributes.\n\nTo do this, look for attributes that depend on another attribute which, in turn, depends on a primary key. For instance, given a table with columns (customer_id, address, city, state, zip_code), where (customer_id, address) is the composite primary key, address might further contain city, state, and zip_code. Here, city relies on address, and zip_code relies on city. These are transitive dependencies.\n\nTo achieve 3NF, you'll need to decompose the original table into two or more tables to eliminate these transitive dependencies, making sure each table contains only attributes that depend uniquely on the primary key. For our example, you would create a separate table for the address information:\n\nNew schema:\n\n1. Customer (customer_id, address)\n2. Address (address, city, state, zip_code)\n\nIn this way, we have separated the transitive dependencies out, thus achieving 3NF. Let me know if you need more insight into",
      "source": "target",
