    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Let's consider a more complex scenario involving Order Details and Products.\n\nSuppose you have the following tables in your database:\n\n1. Order (order_id, customer_id, order_date, employee_id)\n2. Order Details (order_id, product_id, quantity)\n3. Product (product_id, product_name, manufacturer_id)\n4. Manufacturer (manufacturer_id, manufacturer_name, country)\n\nIn this schema, we can have transitive dependencies in the following way:\n\n- Order ties to Order Details through order_id.\n- Order Details binds to Product by product_id.\n- Meanwhile, Product is linked to Manufacturer via manufacturer_id, implying that Manufacturer indirectly impacts Product.\n\nNow we have some identified transitive dependencies:\n\n- product_name depends on manufacturer_name through manufacturer_id.\n\nTo normalize this to 3NF, we'll need to break down the tables by removing the transitive dependencies:\n\n1. Order\n- (order_id, customer_id, order_date, employee_id)\n\n2. Order Details\n- (order_id, product_id, quantity)\n\n3. Product\n- (product_id, product_name, manufacturer_id)\n\n4. Manufacturer\n- (manufacturer_id, manufacturer_name, country)\n\nBy restructuring it this way, we've removed the transitive dependency,",
